{"claims":[{"excerpt":"Not everyone without a job is counted as unemployed.","claim":"Having no job does not by itself mean a person is counted as unemployed.","category":"scope","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"Under the Current Population Survey, an unemployed person was not employed during the survey reference week, was available for work except for temporary illness, and made an active job search effort in the four-week period ending with that week or was temporarily laid off and expected recall.","narrative":"rather than by everyone without a job","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The source defines unemployment using additional conditions beyond not being employed.","materiality":"Material distinction central to the explanation."},{"excerpt":"The unemployment rate’s denominator is the number of employed people plus the number of unemployed people—not everyone who currently has no job.","claim":"The unemployment-rate denominator is employed people plus unemployed people, not everyone without a job.","category":"technical","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"The unemployment rate divides unemployed people by the sum of employed and unemployed people.","narrative":"the combined number of employed and unemployed people, rather than by everyone without a job","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The denominator and contrast match the source.","materiality":"Material definition of the rate’s denominator."},{"excerpt":"To be classified as unemployed, a person generally must not have been employed during the survey’s reference week","claim":"A person generally must not have been employed during the survey reference week to be classified as unemployed.","category":"chronology","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"an unemployed person was not employed during the survey reference week","narrative":"the person must not have been employed during the reference week","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The reference-week condition matches the source; “generally” is consistent with the stated exception to the overall classification rule.","materiality":"Material eligibility condition."},{"excerpt":"must have been available for work (with an exception for temporary illness)","claim":"A person must have been available for work, with temporary illness as an exception.","category":"scope","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"was available for work except for temporary illness","narrative":"must have been available for work, except for temporary illness","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The availability requirement and illness exception match the source.","materiality":"Material eligibility condition."},{"excerpt":"and must have actively looked for work during the four weeks ending with that week.","claim":"A person generally must have actively searched for work during the four-week period ending with the reference week.","category":"chronology","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"made an active job search effort in the four-week period ending with that week or was temporarily laid off and expected recall","narrative":"Active search must fall within the four-week period ending with the reference week","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The search period matches the source, and the following sentence in the piece states the source’s temporary-layoff exception.","materiality":"Material eligibility condition and time window."},{"excerpt":"There’s an exception to the job-search requirement: someone on temporary layoff who expects to be recalled may qualify without actively searching.","claim":"A person on temporary layoff who expects recall may qualify as unemployed without actively searching.","category":"scope","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"or was temporarily laid off and expected recall","narrative":"The first may qualify without an active search","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The exception and its conditions match the source.","materiality":"Material exception to the active-search requirement."},{"excerpt":"But browsing job listings alone doesn’t count as actively looking.","claim":"Browsing job listings alone does not count as active job search.","category":"interpretation","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"Passive-only job search does not count as active search.","narrative":"someone whose only job-search activity was browsing listings. ... passive browsing alone does not meet the active-search requirement.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The example accurately applies the source’s rule about passive-only search.","materiality":"Material clarification of what qualifies as active search."},{"excerpt":"Availability, what happened during the reference week, and job-search activity during the four-week period all affect whether someone is counted as unemployed—and included in the rate’s denominator.","claim":"Availability, employment during the reference week, and job-search activity during the four-week period affect whether a person is classified as unemployed and thus counted in the rate’s denominator.","category":"cause","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"an unemployed person was not employed during the survey reference week, was available for work except for temporary illness, and made an active job search effort in the four-week period ending with that week or was temporarily laid off and expected recall. The unemployment rate divides unemployed people by the sum of employed and unemployed people.","narrative":"availability and the survey reference periods also matter, and that the classification determines whether a person is counted among the unemployed in the rate’s denominator","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The listed conditions determine unemployed classification under the source, and the denominator includes unemployed people.","materiality":"Summarizes the eligibility rules and their relationship to the denominator."}]}

